## Symptom

`signature_invalid` is by far the dominant relay error on xsm, and it never
clears on its own. In the last 200 MB of the connector log:

| error_code | count |
|---|---|
| signature_invalid | 12,593 |
| connection_error | 74 |
| rate_limit_exceeded | 69 |
| contact_unauthorised | 5 |
| nonce_replay | 4 |

By failing op: `heartbeat` 7,770, `poll_inbox` 4,872, `register` 101, `send` 6.

The full error text (truncated in the log at 80 chars; reproduced in full via the
CLI) is:

```
{"ok":false,"error_code":"signature_invalid",
 "error":"verified signer \"codex-midst-vial-6nx9\" does not own session
          (3d08761ae3f3, 01a084dc-c411-7d63-b80f-1081f0637d57)","http_status":403}
```

## Root cause — two halves

### Relay half: "no lease" is reported as "bad signature"

`handle_heartbeat` (`ocaml/relay.ml:5294`) and `handle_inbox_read`
(`ocaml/relay.ml:5747`) both do:

```
match R.alias_of_session relay ~node_id ~session_id with
| Some owner when owner = v -> run ()
| _ -> reject_session_mismatch ~verified:v ~node_id ~session_id
```

`reject_session_mismatch` emits `relay_err_signature_invalid`. The `| _` arm
catches **`None`** as well as a genuine foreign-owner mismatch, and
`alias_of_session` returns `None` both when no row exists and when the row is
`alias_released` (aged past its window).

So three materially different situations — forged signature, lease owned by
someone else, lease absent/expired — are collapsed into one error code that reads
like an authentication failure. A client cannot tell "re-register, your lease is
gone" from "your key is wrong".

### Client half: the connector never re-registers

`C2c_relay_connector.sync` (around `ocaml/c2c_relay_connector.ml:2219`):

```
else if List.mem session_id t.registered then
  ... Relay_client.heartbeat ...   (* on failure: session stays in t.registered *)
else
  ... Relay_client.register ...
```

A session enters `t.registered` on a successful register and only ever leaves it
via `retain_eligible_registered` (i.e. when the **local** session stops being
Alive). A failing heartbeat or poll does not remove it. The connector therefore
heartbeats a lease it does not own forever, and never takes the one action that
would repair it.

## Why this is load-bearing, not cosmetic

`sync_made_progress result` is `result.last_error = None || result.rate_limited`.
A single permanently-failing alias makes **every** pass for that broker root
count as no-progress, which is precisely what drives the staleness watchdog that
kills the whole connector (see the crash-loop bug). So one stuck alias in one
repo is sufficient to take down machine-wide relay delivery, even against a
completely healthy relay.

## Fix direction

Relay:
- add a distinct error code for "no live lease for this (node_id, session_id)",
  e.g. `lease_not_found`, and return it from the `None` branch;
- keep `signature_invalid` for an actual signer/owner mismatch.

Connector:
- on `lease_not_found` (and on a persistent owner mismatch), drop `session_id`
  from `t.registered` so the next pass re-registers instead of retrying a dead
  lease;
- consider not counting a *recoverable* per-alias error as a whole-root
  no-progress pass.

## Evidence

Full investigation: `.collab/findings/2026-09-17T01-50-00Z-kesa-quip-relay-connector-crashloop.md`

Lane: relay-core, serial order B295->B293->B294->B291->B292->B297 in one worktree (.worktrees/b29x-relay-core) — all six touch ocaml/relay.ml and/or ocaml/c2c_relay_connector.ml; parallel worktrees would conflict. Also align the case-sensitive owner comparison in handle_heartbeat/handle_inbox_read with is_lease_releases lowercase comparison (aliases are case-insensitive per repo rules).